WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.266 --> 00:00:01.634 align:start size:94% position:6%
-You've been doing this
for a while.

00:00:01.634 --> 00:00:03.803 size:84% position:42%
You've been covering
Washington for a while.

00:00:03.803 --> 00:00:04.604 line:92%
A lot of people say this is

00:00:04.604 --> 00:00:06.072 align:start size:94% position:6%
the most unprecedented time
we're living in.

00:00:06.072 --> 00:00:08.775
The last couple of days --
voting on a health-care bill

00:00:08.775 --> 00:00:09.642 line:92%
that nobody knows what's in it.

00:00:09.642 --> 00:00:12.846 align:start size:94% position:6%
Is this as unprecedented
as we've been led to believe?

00:00:12.846 --> 00:00:14.147 size:84% position:42% line:92%
-It's wild. It's crazy.

00:00:14.147 --> 00:00:17.317 size:84% position:42%
I mean, nobody knows
what they're voting on.

00:00:17.317 --> 00:00:18.585 align:start size:94% position:6%
They don't know how much
it's going to cost,

00:00:18.585 --> 00:00:21.154
how many people are going to
get knocked off of health care,

00:00:21.154 --> 00:00:23.123 align:start size:94% position:6%
who's gonna get it,
who isn't gonna get it.

00:00:23.123 --> 00:00:27.160
They don't have a score
from the budget office, the CBO,

00:00:27.160 --> 00:00:28.194 size:78% position:39% line:92%
which is nonpartisan.

00:00:28.194 --> 00:00:29.963 align:start size:94% position:6%
And so they're just
going through these bills

00:00:29.963 --> 00:00:32.899
trying to get something
to say, "We didn't fail."

00:00:32.899 --> 00:00:34.434 align:start size:94% position:6%
-And, you know,
while this is happening

00:00:34.434 --> 00:00:37.270
and this is something that's
gonna affect so many people

00:00:37.270 --> 00:00:39.806 align:start size:94% position:6%
and so many people's
health insurance,

00:00:39.806 --> 00:00:40.607 size:81% position:41% line:92%
which is a big deal --

00:00:40.607 --> 00:00:41.941 align:start size:94% position:6%
And then you have something
like this morning

00:00:41.941 --> 00:00:43.176
where all of a sudden, you know,
he starts tweeting

00:00:43.176 --> 00:00:46.179 align:start size:94% position:6%
about how transgender
people are not gonna be
allowed in the military.

00:00:46.179 --> 00:00:48.314 line:92%
I have heard that the Pentagon

00:00:48.314 --> 00:00:50.850 align:start size:94% position:6%
was completely
taken by surprise by this.

00:00:50.850 --> 00:00:52.585 align:start size:94% position:6%
Is that what your reporting
has led to?

00:00:52.585 --> 00:00:54.220 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah.
I mean, it's a little scary

00:00:54.220 --> 00:00:56.523 align:start size:94% position:6%
that the Pentagon, you know,
U.S. Intelligence

00:00:56.523 --> 00:00:58.625
did not know that the president
was gonna change

00:00:58.625 --> 00:01:00.326 line:92%
the policy for transpeople.

00:01:00.326 --> 00:01:03.730 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I don't think that
the Defense Secretary knew.

00:01:03.730 --> 00:01:05.832 size:81% position:41%
He is away on vacation
for a couple of days.

00:01:05.832 --> 00:01:08.735 align:start size:94% position:6%
We've got a lot
of big military issues.

00:01:08.735 --> 00:01:11.371 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Do you think he spoke
to the generals?

00:01:11.371 --> 00:01:12.138 line:92%
I know he was saying that.

00:01:12.138 --> 00:01:14.407
Is that something that we can be
led to believe is true?

00:01:14.407 --> 00:01:16.843 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Don't know. Can't say.
-Yeah.

00:01:16.843 --> 00:01:18.011 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
-But the fact is

00:01:18.011 --> 00:01:20.213 align:start size:94% position:6%
that the generals
have other big things --

00:01:20.213 --> 00:01:21.448 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
Afghanistan, Syria,

00:01:21.448 --> 00:01:23.349 align:start size:94% position:6%
you know, how to deal
with North Korea,

00:01:23.349 --> 00:01:25.151 align:start size:94% position:6%
whether missiles
are going to go up

00:01:25.151 --> 00:01:27.220 line:92%
as early as tonight or tomorrow.

00:01:27.220 --> 00:01:30.090 align:start size:94% position:6%
-As early as tonight?
-Yeah, maybe.

00:01:30.090 --> 00:01:32.992 size:81% position:41%
-Oh, I hope they wait
until after this airs.

00:01:32.992 --> 00:01:35.995 align:start size:94% position:6%
I'd love to get one more in.
-One more in.

00:01:35.995 --> 00:01:39.365
-And then, I mean, there's a lot
of theories floating around.

00:01:39.365 --> 00:01:41.401 align:start size:94% position:6%
Do you think
that this is something,

00:01:41.401 --> 00:01:43.303 align:start size:94% position:6%
an issue that is near and dear
to his heart

00:01:43.303 --> 00:01:45.638
or that he's playing to his base
or that he's using it

00:01:45.638 --> 00:01:47.440 align:start size:94% position:6%
to distract from
the health-care issue

00:01:47.440 --> 00:01:48.241 line:92%
that people talk about less

00:01:48.241 --> 00:01:50.043 align:start size:94% position:6%
when he does a tweet
about transgender soldiers?

00:01:50.043 --> 00:01:52.979 align:start size:94% position:6%
-It's hard to say
what's going on in his mind,

00:01:52.979 --> 00:01:54.848 line:92%
you know, what he's thinking.

00:01:54.848 --> 00:01:57.417 align:start size:94% position:6%
I'm -- Seriously,
I can't interpret it,

00:01:57.417 --> 00:01:59.752 align:start size:94% position:6%
but it certainly
has been a distraction

00:01:59.752 --> 00:02:02.155 line:92%
from the failure on health care.

00:02:02.155 --> 00:02:03.456 line:92%
It does play to the base.

00:02:03.456 --> 00:02:05.959 align:start size:94% position:6%
It's a social issue,
and it contradicts completely

00:02:05.959 --> 00:02:07.460 align:start size:94% position:6%
what he said
during the campaign.

00:02:07.460 --> 00:02:10.230
So it's hard to figure out that
this is near and dear to him

00:02:10.230 --> 00:02:12.332 size:78% position:39%
if it's not something
he was talking about

00:02:12.332 --> 00:02:13.533 line:92%
when he was running for office.

00:02:13.533 --> 00:02:15.368 align:start size:94% position:6%
-One of the things,
covering foreign policy,

00:02:15.368 --> 00:02:18.204
you have to cover the Secretary
of State, Rex Tillerson.

00:02:18.204 --> 00:02:20.507 align:start size:94% position:6%
He has not been taking
a lot of questions.

00:02:20.507 --> 00:02:21.875 line:92%
Would you say this is the least?

00:02:21.875 --> 00:02:22.575 size:75% position:38% line:92%
-Uh, yes. [ Laughs ]

00:02:22.575 --> 00:02:24.844 align:start size:94% position:6%
-In your time, there's never
been a Secretary of State

00:02:24.844 --> 00:02:26.746 line:92%
that's allowed less access?

00:02:26.746 --> 00:02:27.413 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
-Absolutely.

00:02:27.413 --> 00:02:30.483 align:start size:94% position:6%
-And you've tried very hard,
very noble efforts.

00:02:30.483 --> 00:02:31.317 size:84% position:42% line:92%
Here you are asking him

00:02:31.317 --> 00:02:33.052 align:start size:94% position:6%
some questions
at the end of a photo op.

00:02:33.052 --> 00:02:35.221 line:92%
Let's take a look real quick.

00:02:41.327 --> 00:02:43.963 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Thank you!
-Thank you!

00:02:46.566 --> 00:02:48.468 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Thank you, guys.
We're leaving.

00:02:48.468 --> 00:02:51.304 line:92%
-Thank you. Andrea. Andrea.

00:02:55.008 --> 00:02:56.242 size:88% position:44% line:92%
-The press is departing.

00:02:56.242 --> 00:02:58.545 align:start size:94% position:6%
-We haven't had
any time in here.

00:02:58.545 --> 00:02:59.679 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
-I'm so sorry.

00:02:59.679 --> 00:03:01.581 align:start size:94% position:6%
-[ Laughs ]
-Oh, God.

00:03:01.581 --> 00:03:03.183 size:84% position:42% line:92%
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:03.183 --> 00:03:04.551 size:81% position:41% line:92%
-I'm -- You know what?

00:03:04.551 --> 00:03:07.754 align:start size:94% position:6%
-I mean, that is humiliating.
-That, yeah.

00:03:07.754 --> 00:03:08.588 size:84% position:42% line:92%
You looked like a woman

00:03:08.588 --> 00:03:10.657
who was lost in a shopping mall
at the end.

00:03:10.657 --> 00:03:13.593 align:start size:94% position:6%
Also, it's interesting
when people say "thank you,"

00:03:13.593 --> 00:03:16.296 align:start size:94% position:6%
and you get the sense
they're not that thankful.

00:03:16.296 --> 00:03:18.298 line:92%
And, you know, the other thing

00:03:18.298 --> 00:03:20.967 size:84% position:42%
is he is not letting
the press fly with him.

00:03:20.967 --> 00:03:22.702 align:start size:94% position:6%
And here is a guy
who is taking trips

00:03:22.702 --> 00:03:25.939 align:start size:94% position:6%
to obviously
everywhere across the globe,

00:03:25.939 --> 00:03:26.639 size:78% position:39% line:92%
and so you have to --

00:03:26.639 --> 00:03:29.275
The press has to fly commercial
to try to catch up with him.

00:03:29.275 --> 00:03:30.944 align:start size:94% position:6%
Has that made your job
even harder?

00:03:30.944 --> 00:03:34.013
-It's impossible because you
cannot fly from here to there,

00:03:34.013 --> 00:03:37.483
from country to country,
without a military plane.

00:03:37.483 --> 00:03:38.284 align:start size:94% position:6%
I don't have one.
-Yeah.

00:03:38.284 --> 00:03:42.388
-And we pay our way on this,
so this is not a budget issue.

00:03:42.388 --> 00:03:43.323 line:92%
They say it's a budget issue.

00:03:43.323 --> 00:03:46.226 align:start size:94% position:6%
But he doesn't want to have
the press corps around.

00:03:46.226 --> 00:03:47.360 line:92%
So he has one person with him --

00:03:47.360 --> 00:03:49.896 align:start size:94% position:6%
one person from
the media now that --

00:03:49.896 --> 00:03:52.432 line:92%
He sort of represents all of us.

00:03:52.432 --> 00:03:55.168 align:start size:94% position:6%
But the fact is that
for decades now

00:03:55.168 --> 00:03:57.570 align:start size:94% position:6%
Secretaries of State
have briefed us on the plane

00:03:57.570 --> 00:04:00.073 align:start size:94% position:6%
or had their top officials
briefing us.

00:04:00.073 --> 00:04:01.574 align:start size:94% position:6%
He doesn't have
that many top officials

00:04:01.574 --> 00:04:03.443 align:start size:94% position:6%
'cause they haven't
filled the vacancies,

00:04:03.443 --> 00:04:04.811 line:92%
which is another whole issue.

00:04:04.811 --> 00:04:07.513 align:start size:94% position:6%
But it really is hurting
American diplomacy.

00:04:07.513 --> 00:04:10.149 align:start size:94% position:6%
It's not good
for us as a country

00:04:10.149 --> 00:04:12.485 align:start size:94% position:6%
to not have the press
able to say,

00:04:12.485 --> 00:04:13.853 line:92%
"Well, this is what the goal is

00:04:13.853 --> 00:04:16.055 align:start size:94% position:6%
of this administration
or this secretary."

00:04:16.055 --> 00:04:18.258 align:start size:94% position:6%
It also sends
a really bad signal, I think,

00:04:18.258 --> 00:04:21.894 size:75% position:38%
to regimes in China,
in Turkey, in Russia

00:04:21.894 --> 00:04:25.365
where reporters don't only
not get press conferences.

00:04:25.365 --> 00:04:28.101 align:start size:94% position:6%
They get locked up.
They get killed.

00:04:28.101 --> 00:04:30.436 align:start size:94% position:6%
I mean, there has always
been a tradition

00:04:30.436 --> 00:04:31.604 align:start size:94% position:6%
where the
U.S. Secretary of State,

00:04:31.604 --> 00:04:34.907
Republicans and Democrats,
bring the press corps along.

00:04:34.907 --> 00:04:37.076 size:88% position:44%
And if they don't have
press conferences there,

00:04:37.076 --> 00:04:40.413 align:start size:94% position:6%
they --- he or she
will hold a press conference

00:04:40.413 --> 00:04:43.516 size:88% position:44%
to show that we believe
in freedom of the press.

00:04:43.516 --> 00:04:44.784 line:92%
-That is very disturbing.

00:04:44.784 --> 00:04:46.586 size:84% position:42%
I mentioned you've been
doing this a long time,

00:04:46.586 --> 00:04:49.155 align:start size:94% position:6%
and you are
sort of a trailblazer

00:04:49.155 --> 00:04:50.356 size:84% position:42% line:92%
as a female journalist.

00:04:50.356 --> 00:04:53.760 align:start size:94% position:6%
And very early on, you noticed
a bit of the distinction

00:04:53.760 --> 00:04:57.864 align:start size:94% position:6%
that people in charge
will make between female
and male reporters.

00:04:57.864 --> 00:04:58.931 line:92%
You covered Three Mile Island,

00:04:58.931 --> 00:05:02.201 align:start size:94% position:6%
which was obviously
a big story.

00:05:02.201 --> 00:05:03.436 size:81% position:41% line:92%
And what was your beat

00:05:03.436 --> 00:05:05.905 size:84% position:42%
that you were in charge
of Three Mile Island?

00:05:05.905 --> 00:05:09.042 align:start size:94% position:6%
-I covered -- I was
the NBC energy correspondent,

00:05:09.042 --> 00:05:10.376 line:92%
so I covered nuclear energy,

00:05:10.376 --> 00:05:12.578 align:start size:94% position:6%
and this was
a potential meltdown.

00:05:12.578 --> 00:05:13.613 size:81% position:41% line:92%
So it was on my watch.

00:05:13.613 --> 00:05:15.248 align:start size:94% position:6%
-And so what happened
when you tried

00:05:15.248 --> 00:05:16.482 align:start size:94% position:6%
to go report from
Three Mile Island?

00:05:16.482 --> 00:05:20.720 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Well, a very kindly,
paternalistic bureau chief,

00:05:20.720 --> 00:05:25.191 align:start size:94% position:6%
a lovely man said, when
I noticed that five days in,

00:05:25.191 --> 00:05:26.559 line:92%
I was the only correspondent

00:05:26.559 --> 00:05:29.329 align:start size:94% position:6%
not going in
and covering this thing --

00:05:29.329 --> 00:05:31.764 align:start size:94% position:6%
I went and said,
"How come I'm the only one

00:05:31.764 --> 00:05:33.866 align:start size:94% position:6%
who's not going
to Three Mile Island?"

00:05:33.866 --> 00:05:36.102 align:start size:94% position:6%
And he said,
"Because you are a woman

00:05:36.102 --> 00:05:37.870 size:81% position:41% line:92%
of child-bearing age."

00:05:37.870 --> 00:05:38.504 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
-Wow.

00:05:38.504 --> 00:05:42.642 align:start size:94% position:6%
-And I said,
"Has it occurred to you

00:05:42.642 --> 00:05:46.245 align:start size:94% position:6%
that men's balls
are as vulnerable

00:05:46.245 --> 00:05:49.782 align:start size:94% position:6%
to radiation
as women's ovaries?"

00:05:49.782 --> 00:05:51.150 size:84% position:42% line:92%
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:05:51.150 --> 00:05:54.053 line:92%
-That's great. It's very --

00:05:54.053 --> 00:05:55.121 align:start size:94% position:6%
-I mean,
I got there the next day.

00:05:55.121 --> 00:05:57.557 align:start size:94% position:6%
They sent me right out.
-You got there the next day.

00:05:57.557 --> 00:05:59.926 align:start size:94% position:6%
Also, I want to say
that's very noble

00:05:59.926 --> 00:06:00.927 line:92%
because for all the doors

00:06:00.927 --> 00:06:02.695 align:start size:94% position:6%
that weren't open to women
for so long,

00:06:02.695 --> 00:06:05.365
the one that you would think
they'd be happy to have closed

00:06:05.365 --> 00:06:07.033 align:start size:94% position:6%
is the door
to Three Mile Island.

00:06:07.033 --> 00:06:08.935 align:start size:94% position:6%
But you --
I think that speaks a lot

00:06:08.935 --> 00:06:11.003 align:start size:94% position:6%
to the integrity you have
as a journalist.

00:06:11.003 --> 00:06:12.705 align:start size:94% position:6%
And please keep up
the good work, Andrea.

00:06:12.705 --> 00:06:13.606 align:start size:94% position:6%
It's just lovely
having you here.

00:06:13.606 --> 00:06:15.508 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Thank you, Seth.
-Andrea Mitchell everybody.

00:06:15.508 --> 00:06:19.278 align:start size:94% position:6%
"Andrea Mitchell Reports" airs
weekdays at noon on MSNBC.

